[38;2;124;124;124mfinitely generated module M is reflexive iff M is torsion-free and satisfies [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(S_2). MCM modules satisfy depth M_p ≥ min(2, dim A_p) for all p... actually MCM[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmeans depth M_p = dim A_p for all p (or depth M = dim A globally, and it [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpropagates). For a MCM module, depth_{A_p} M_p = dim A_p ≥ min(2, ht p) when ht [0m[38;2;124;124;124mp ≥ 1... Let me think. M is MCM means depth M = dim A. Localization: M_p is MCM [0m[38;2;124;124;124mover A_p. So depth_{A_p} M_p = dim A_p. For (S_2), we need depth_{A_p} M_p ≥ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmin(2, dim A_p). Since depth = dim A_p ≥ min(2, dim A_p). ✓ So M satisfies [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(S_2). M is torsion-free (MCM over domain → torsion-free, since torsion would [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mreduce depth). So M is reflexive. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m5. Rank-1 reflexive modules ↔ Cl(A). ✓ This is the standard theory (Bourbaki, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFossum's book). The map: a height-1 prime p gives the reflexive module A ∩ (1/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mp)... or the divisorial ideal. The class group Cl(A) = Div(A)/Prin(A) where Div [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis free abelian group on height-1 primes. A rank-1 reflexive module M [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcorresponds to a divisorial ideal, which gives a Weil divisor, whose class is in[0m[38;2;124;124;124mCl(A). Two rank-1 reflexive modules are isomorphic iff their classes are equal. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mM ≅ A iff class is 0. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m6. UFD iff Cl(A) = 0. ✓ Standard. In a UFD, every height-1 prime is principal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(generated by a prime element), so Cl = 0. Conversely, Cl = 0 means every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mheight-1 prime is principal, which for a noetherian domain means UFD [0m
